Title: Diagnosis
Details: The failure of the test `testSingleVariableAndConstraint` and the issue report about incorrect detection of basic variables in the simplex tableau are related to the method `getBasicRow(int col)` in `SimplexTableau`. This method is responsible for identifying the row that corresponds to the basic variable for the given column in the tableau.

Examining the code of `getBasicRow(int)`:
```java
272 :     private Integer getBasicRow(final int col) {
273 :         Integer row = null;
274 :         for (int i = getNumObjectiveFunctions(); i < getHeight(); i++) {
275 :             if (!MathUtils.equals(getEntry(i, col), 0.0, epsilon)) {
276 :                 if (row == null) {
277 :                 row = i;
278 :                 } else {
279 :                 return null;
280 :                 }
281 :             }
282 :         }
283 :         return row;
284 :     }
```
This method currently identifies a basic row by checking if the tableau entry at `(i, col)` is not equal to zero (within epsilon). The bug report explicitly states that this logic is flawed because a basic variable is identified not simply when the value is nonzero, but specifically when the entry equals 1.0 in that column (also meaning there is exactly one such entry per column). 

Because the current method considers any nonzero value indicative of a basic variable, it can mistakenly fail to identify basic variables correctly when the entry should be exactly 1. This leads to the simplex algorithm not finding the correct basic variables, hence failing optimizations like the simple single variable test case.

The initialization method and subtraction functions confirm the tableau structure adheres to the standard simplex tableau construction, so the root cause lies in basic variable detection via `getBasicRow`.

Correcting `getBasicRow` to check for entries exactly equal to 1.0 (within epsilon) and ensuring only one such entry exists per column will fix the issue.

No evidence in recent unrelated bug reports contradicts or further informs this diagnosis.

Summary: The test fails due to the incorrect logic in `SimplexTableau.getBasicRow` which treats any nonzero entry as a basic variable indicator, rather than specifically an entry of value 1. This causes the simplex solver to fail in identifying the basic variables correctly and thus fail optimization.

The fault lies specifically in the method `SimplexTableau.getBasicRow(int)`.

